<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Black Sea Fleet</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1783">1783</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> Fleet is the Russian navy's fleet in the Black Sea, founded in 1783 after the annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> and based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sevastopol">Sevastopol</a>. In the revolution of 1917–1918 its sailors were a radical Bolshevik force that helped overrun Crimea. In June 1918 many of its ships were scuttled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novorossiysk">Novorossiysk</a> to keep them from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/black-sea-fleet.jpg" alt="Black Sea Fleet" width="320" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: w:Black Sea Fleet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_emblem_of_the_Black_Sea_fleet.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> tried to expand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and set up a naval base on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-azov">Sea of Azov</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1686-1700">Russo-Turkish War</a> of 1695–96, but he had to surrender those gains after the war of 1710 to 1713. Russia's position in the south advanced slowly after the war of 1735 to 1739 and then much faster under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a>, whose Greek Project envisaged partitioning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> between Russia and the Habsburg Empire. Beginning with the war of 1768–1774, Russia and the Ottoman Empire fought seven more wars up to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> over which would dominate the Black Sea region. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grigory-potemkin">Prince Potemkin</a> founded the fleet on 13 May 1783 as part of the Imperial Russian Navy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Russian SFSR inherited the fleet in 1918, and with the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1922 it became part of the Soviet Navy. After the defeat of the anti-Bolshevik <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armed-forces-of-south-russia">Armed Forces of South Russia</a> the Soviet government took control of all naval elements. The few remaining ships were scrapped in the 1920s, and a large construction programme began in the 1930s, at first defensive and based on small submarines, then from about 1935 with cruisers and destroyers. Under the Montreaux Convention of 1936 the Soviet Union secured the peacetime right for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> ships to pass the Turkish Straits.</p><p>With the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> the fleet lost its major missions and much of its funding. In the early 1990s it had 645 ships and 72,000 personnel. In 1992 the greater part of its personnel, weapons and coastal facilities came under the jurisdiction of newly independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, which ordered its own navy to be formed from it, and several ships declared themselves Ukrainian. This led to conflicts with the many officers loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. In 1997 the fleet was partitioned between Russia and Ukraine, and Russia received title to 82 per cent of the vessels.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The fleet has its main headquarters and facilities at the Sevastopol Naval Base in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>. Its use of leased bases there was sometimes controversial in the years after 1991, for example over the refusal to let <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukrainians">Ukrainians</a> inspect aircraft cargo and the occupation of the Sarych lighthouse from 2005.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Black Sea Fleet” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Crew of the Russian <b>Black Sea Fleet</b> battleship rose against their officers after a dispute over rotten meat.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-27">27 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The republic was run by a council of commissars at Novorossiysk, relying on sailors of the <b>Black Sea Fleet</b> and local Red Guards.</blockquote><a href="/state/black-sea-soviet-republic">Black Sea Soviet Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>In January 1918 Bolshevik forces and sailors of the <b>Black Sea Fleet</b> overran the Crimea.</blockquote><a href="/state/crimean-peoples-republic">Crimean People's Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>In June 1918, on Lenin's orders, much of the <b>Black Sea Fleet</b> was scuttled in the bay of Novorossiysk to prevent its capture by Germany.</blockquote><a href="/state/kuban-black-sea-soviet-republic">Kuban-Black Sea Soviet Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>The republic relied on Red Guards and <b>Black Sea Fleet</b> sailors, imposed levies on the wealthy and carried out arrests and executions.</blockquote><a href="/state/odessa-soviet-republic">Odessa Soviet Republic</a></li></ul></section>
